<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Writing your own macros>

  When you often type the same text, or the same combination of text and
  formatting, you may teach it to <TeXmacs> by defining a <em|macro>. A
  macro has a name; each time you use the name, <TeXmacs> replaces it by the
  text which you associated with it. Macros may also have <em|arguments>,
  which are filled in each time you use them. All the tags which we met in
  the previous chapters, such as <markup|em>, <markup|section> or
  <markup|theorem>, are actually macros defined in the style files of
  <TeXmacs>, and your own macros are used in exactly the same way.

  <\traverse>
    <branch|A first macro|tut-new-macro.en.tm>

    <branch|Macros with arguments and the preamble|tut-macro-args.en.tm>

    <branch|Reusing your macros in other documents|tut-style-package.en.tm>
